## Variables

Built-in variables — the heart of awk

$0
ENTIRE LINE
The complete current line being processed
awk '{print $0}' file → prints all lines

$1 $2 $N
FIELD NUMBER
Individual field values. $1=first, $2=second, and so on
awk '{print $1}' file → first column

NF
NUMBER OF FIELDS
Total fields in current line. $NF = last field always
awk '{print $NF}' file → last column

NR
NUMBER OF RECORDS
Current line number being processed (starts at 1)
awk '{print NR, $0}' file → add line numbers

FS
FIELD SEPARATOR
Input delimiter. Default=whitespace. Same as -F flag
awk 'BEGIN{FS=":"}{print $1}' /etc/passwd

OFS
OUTPUT FIELD SEP
Separator between fields in print output. Default=space
awk 'BEGIN{OFS=","}{print $1,$2}' file

RS
RECORD SEPARATOR
Input record separator. Default=newline. Change to process multi-line records
awk 'BEGIN{RS=","}{print $0}' file

ORS
OUTPUT RECORD SEP
Separator after each print statement. Default=newline
awk 'BEGIN{ORS="\n---\n"}{print}' file

FNR
FILE RECORD NUMBER
Line number within current file. Resets to 1 for each file. NR keeps counting across files
awk '{print FNR, $0}' file1 file2

FILENAME
CURRENT FILE
Name of the file currently being processed
awk '{print FILENAME, $0}' *.log

ARGC ARGV
ARGUMENT COUNT/LIST
Number of args and array of argument values
awk 'BEGIN{print ARGC}' file

myvar
USER VARIABLE
You can create your own variables. No declaration needed. Starts as 0 or ""
awk '{sum+=$3} END{print sum}' file

Most used variables — memorize these
$1 $2 $NF → field values
NR → line number
NF → number of fields
FS → input delimiter (or use -F flag)
OFS → output delimiter
